--------------------------------\n[2511.15503v1] A Tensor Compiler for Processing-In-Memory Architectures (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15503v1)\nciteturn2479view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15503v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Hardware Architecture\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 19 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 28 Aug 2026 (cite5†v5 )]\nL9: # Title:A Tensor Compiler for Processing-In-Memory Architectures\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Peiming Yang , cite7†Sankeerth Durvasula , cite8†Ivan Fernandez , cite9†Mohammad Sadrosadati , cite10†Onur Mutlu , cite11†Gennady Pekhimenko , cite12†Christina Giannoula L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled A Tensor Compiler for Processing-In-Memory Architectures, by Peiming Yang and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Processing-In-Memory (PIM) devices integrated with high-performance Host processors (e.g., GPUs) can accelerate memory-intensive kernels in Machine Learning (ML) models, including Large Language Models (LLMs), by leveraging high memory bandwidth at PIM cores. However, Host processors and PIM cores require different data layouts: Hosts need consecutive elements distributed across DRAM banks, while PIM cores need them within local banks.\nL17: This necessitates data rearrangements in ML kernel execution that pose significant performance and programmability challenges, further exacerbated by the need to support diverse PIM backends. Current compilation approaches lack systematic optimization for diverse ML kernels across multiple PIM backends and may largely ignore data rearrangements during compute code optimization.\nL18: We demonstrate that data rearrangements and compute code optimization are interdependent, and need to be jointly optimized during the tuning process. To address this, we design DCC, the first data-centric ML compiler for PIM systems that jointly co-optimizes data rearrangements and compute code in a unified tuning process. DCC integrates a multi-layer PIM abstraction that enables various data distribution and processing strategies on different PIM backends.\nL19: DCC enables effective co-optimization by mapping data partitioning strategies to compute loop partitions, applying PIM-specific code optimizations and leveraging a fast and accurate performance prediction model to select optimal configurations. Our evaluations in various individual ML kernels demonstrate that DCC achieves up to 7.68x speedup (2.7x average) on HBM-PIM and up to 13.17x speedup (5.75x average) on AttAcc PIM backend over GPU-only execution.\nL20: In end-to-end LLM inference, DCC on AttAcc accelerates GPT-3 and LLaMA-2 by up to 7.71x (4.88x average) over GPU.\nL21: Subjects:  | Hardware Architecture (cs.AR); Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Performance (cs.PF)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite15†arXiv:2511.15503 [cs.AR]\nL23:    | (or cite16†arXiv:2511.15503v1 [cs.AR] for this version)\nL24:    | cite17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.15503†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Christina Giannoula [cite18†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 19 Nov 2025 14:58:16 UTC (663 KB)\nL29: cite19†[v2] Fri, 22 May 2026 15:57:54 UTC (1,086 KB)\nL30: cite20†[v3] Sat, 27 Jun 2026 12:43:41 UTC (1,421 KB)\nL31: cite21†[v4] Sun, 2 Aug 2026 14:08:33 UTC (1,604 KB)\nL32: cite5†[v5] Fri, 28 Aug 2026 20:47:55 UTC (1,604 KB)\nL33: \nL34: Full-text links:\nL35: ## Access Paper:\nL36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled A Tensor Compiler for Processing-In-Memory 